[00:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] &gt;&gt; All right, YouTube. What is good? It\'s your boy Rebel back with another video and today we are taking a look at Krakoa Imagine. Krakoa Imagine is a fine-tune'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of Krakoa 2 Turbo built around a Krakoa Imagine derived style adaptation released in into eight and full guff letters, so it runs on as low as eight'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gigabyte VRAM cards. Now, what you will actually see from this fine-tune is lighting changes, detail density, and aesthetics. So, the lighting is the most pronounced difference being that it'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'provides much more aggressive light shaping, harder key lights, stronger falloff, deeper contrast ratios, and more willingness to let regions go fully black and blow out. The detail density is a lot more noticeable on micro'

[00:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'details like surfaces, fabrics, skin, and foliage. The model does well at resolutions anywhere from 1024 to 1536, but going past the 1536 does cause some'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'slight artifacting or hallucination based on seed. And the aesthetics are very prompt dependent but prominent. So, Krakoa Imagine has very heavy aesthetic value behind it and the data set that I'

[01:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'curated for this fine-tune was very much so aesthetic heavy in extremely varying aesthetic choices anywhere from cyberpunk to dream-like to nightmare'

[01:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scenes to photorealistic photography. All of the, you know, basic and more obscure aesthetics are present inside this data set. So, now like I stated'

[01:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'before, it is available in into eight com route or you can choose a guff anywhere from Q2 to Q8. The workflows are also both in the repo, so you can grab your model of choice. And one thing'

[02:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to remember is this is a fine-tune of Korea, so you will need the Korea 2 text encoder and VAE of your choice. If you choose a higher or lower text encoder, but after that you can grab the model'

[02:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'files and the workflow, throw it in comfy, and it will look something like this. So, this is just a simple subgraph that I created hosting all of your model files here. And the only difference'

[02:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between the two will be the unit name being N8 or Guff. Everything else is exactly the same. This here is just my matrix monitor that monitors your step counts. I just have it in the workflow'

[02:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cuz I was testing the model. So, you have your positive prompt, you have your negative prompt. At the top, you have your seed. If you would like to run a fixed seed, you\'ll need to pull this string off to change your control. You have your step'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'count, your CFG, you have your sampler and scheduler if you would like to change those. And here you have your height and your width. Value is your height and value one is your width. You have your clip, your VAE, and you have'

[03:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your model. And also inside the workflow is the conditioning rebalance node just to help bypass some of the filter problems that will be present in some of the images you\'ll want to generate with'

[03:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this. So, I do have some examples for you guys today, and we will be taking a look at those now. So, each of these three model cards were all generated with the model. Just three different looks that I wanted to showcase as these'

[03:30] [scene:talk]
